Matching on Different Errors
The code in Listing 9-4 will panic! no matter why File::open failed. However, we want to
take different actions for different failure reasons. If File::open failed because the file doesn’t
exist, we want to create the file and return the handle to the new file. If File::open failed for
any other reason—for example, because we didn’t have permission to open the file—we still
want the code to panic! in the same way it did in Listing 9-4. For this, we add an inner match
expression, shown in Listing 9-5.
Filename: src/main.rs
Listing 9-5: Handling different kinds of errors in different ways
The type of the value that File::open returns inside the Err variant is io::Error , which is a
struct provided by the standard library. This struct has a method kind that we can call to get
